    body: [
      { type: "p", text: "Two companies can reimburse an employee the exact same amount for the exact same trip, and one payment is tax-free while the other is taxed as ordinary wages. The difference isn't the amount — it's whether the reimbursement arrangement qualifies as an IRS accountable plan." },
      { type: "callout", title: "TL;DR", text: "An IRS accountable plan is a reimbursement arrangement that meets a three-part test — genuine business connection, timely substantiation, and return of any excess advance — and reimbursements that pass all three are tax-free to the employee and deductible to the employer. Fail the test, and the entire reimbursement becomes taxable wages under a 'non-accountable plan.'" },
      { type: "h2", text: "What an accountable plan actually is" },
      { type: "p", text: "An IRS accountable plan is a set of expense-reimbursement rules, grounded in Internal Revenue Code Section 162 and its Treasury regulations, that keeps travel payments tax-free for both the employer and the employee. It isn't a document you file with the IRS — it's a standard your reimbursement process either meets or doesn't, trip by trip. Without it, reimbursements are treated as taxable wages, no different from salary." },
      { type: "h2", text: "The three-part accountable plan test" },
      { type: "ul", items: [
        "Business connection: the expense must be for a genuine business purpose — not personal travel disguised as a work trip.",
        "Substantiation: the employee must submit documentation (receipts, mileage log, expense report) showing the amount, date, place and purpose within a reasonable time — the IRS treats 60 days from when the expense was incurred as a safe-harbour window.",
        "Return of excess: any amount paid in advance that exceeds actual expenses must be returned within a reasonable time, generally within 120 days of when the excess was identified.",
      ] },
      { type: "table", caption: "Accountable vs non-accountable plan", headers: ["", "Accountable plan", "Non-accountable plan"], rows: [
        ["Business connection required", "Yes", "Not required"],
        ["Substantiation required", "Yes, within a reasonable time", "Not required"],
        ["Excess must be returned", "Yes", "Not required"],
        ["Tax treatment to employee", "Tax-free", "Taxable wages"],
        ["Employer payroll tax exposure", "None", "Subject to payroll tax"],
      ] },
      { type: "h2", text: "What happens without an accountable plan" },
      { type: "p", text: "A \"non-accountable plan\" — where employees receive a flat travel allowance with no requirement to document or return excess, such as an unstructured travel stipend — makes the entire allowance taxable compensation subject to income tax and payroll taxes. See our guide to [what is a travel stipend?](/blog/what-is-a-travel-stipend) for how a poorly-structured stipend commonly falls into exactly this trap. The employer also loses the ability to treat the payment cleanly as a non-taxable reimbursed business expense in payroll reporting." },
      { type: "quote", text: "An arrangement will be treated as satisfying the business connection, substantiation, and returning amounts requirements... if it meets the requirements of paragraphs (b), (c), and (d) of this section.", attribution: "Treasury Regulation §1.62-2, accountable plan requirements, irs.gov" },
      { type: "h2", text: "Per diem under an accountable plan" },
      { type: "p", text: "When you use the GSA per diem rates and employees file expense reports showing the destination, dates and business purpose, the reimbursement is automatically within the accountable plan framework — this is one of per diem's biggest practical advantages over ad-hoc reimbursement. You do not need lodging receipts to satisfy the substantiation requirement for M&IE — only evidence of the trip itself, as we cover in [is per diem taxable?](/blog/is-per-diem-taxable)." },
      { type: "h2", text: "How to build a compliant policy" },
      { type: "p", text: "Write an expense reimbursement policy that requires employees to submit a report with the four substantiation points (date, destination, business purpose, amount) within a defined window, no later than the IRS's 60-day safe harbour. Use the GSA per diem rates as your daily ceiling, and require any pre-paid advances to be reconciled with excess returned promptly. Our [per diem calculator](/calculators/per-diem-calculator) produces itemised, rate-backed trip totals that fit neatly into this documentation workflow, giving both the traveller and finance a clean paper trail." },
      { type: "faq", items: [
        { q: "What is an IRS accountable plan?", a: "A reimbursement arrangement that meets three requirements — a genuine business connection, timely substantiation, and return of any excess advance — allowing the reimbursement to be tax-free to the employee." },
        { q: "How long do employees have to substantiate an expense?", a: "The IRS treats 60 days from when the expense was incurred as a reasonable safe-harbour period, though many employer policies set a shorter internal deadline." },
        { q: "Does per diem automatically qualify as an accountable plan?", a: "Yes, provided the payment doesn't exceed the federal per diem rate and is backed by a basic trip report showing dates, destination and business purpose." },
        { q: "What happens to unreturned excess advances?", a: "Any advance amount not returned within a reasonable time — generally 120 days — becomes taxable to the employee, even if the original advance was properly structured." },
      ] },
    ],
